A||||12||Homicidal||What happens when a sub box has air leaks?||||1||
Z||000000||Homicidal||05-08-2000||10:55 PM||||I think (actually, I KNOW) my box has some air leaks.  I am going to plug them.  But is it true if it has ANY, even small ones it can affect the bass radically?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||05-08-2000||11:33 PM||||An unsealed box will cause the woofer to distort at the low end freqs and high power range. The pressure created by the woofer on a sealed box acts as a "shock absorber" to dampen the distorion. This of course depends on the quality of woofer you are using. If you look at a "free air" woofer the cone is thicker than a normal woofer. This too helps cut down distorion. A ported box gives you the harmonic chamber to get a lower freq response but your power capabilities are less. Sealed box requires more power than a ported box. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homicidal||05-09-2000||02:48 AM||||It's meant for tight enclosures and it's in a sealed box.  I will of course seal the holes, I was just wondering if it really does degrade performence even with a LITTLE hole.||||reg||1
Z||000003||overlord||05-09-2000||05:38 AM||||ya..i was wondering this tooo...my sub boxes are ported...and as far as i can tell there are only about 2-3 pinhole sized leaks oon the boxes...is there any poiint in plugging these holes? (its real hard to get at)<P>or would my carpet just fix this problem when i put it on?||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||05-09-2000||08:40 AM||||a ported box has WAY different design criteria. and they too need to be completely sealed EXCEPT for the ports themselves. When you do assemble and seal your sealed box if your not satisfied with its low fq extension you can ad Polyfil this will slow down the compression of a in the box thus fooling the sub into thinking it's in a larger box and giving you more useable low frequency extension. Adding your carpet will mask the problem not fix it. just take the time and fix it right so you don't have to mess up the carpet to fix it again.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Homicidal||05-09-2000||10:12 AM||||Will silicone work?  What can I use?<P>BTW, i have around 5 air leaks!||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||05-09-2000||01:41 PM||||yeah that will do fine.. since the box won't be getting wet you might also want to try White Lighting (Latex Chaulk) it's about $0.70 for a BIG tube where silicone is more expensive... if you don't have any of that but you do have rubberized undercoating... spray that on the inside of the box on the seams and with your finger force it into the leaking areas. thats messy and really a last resort but it works if your in a bind... I start off using piloted screw holes in 18mm birch plywood, 2" deck screws (not drywall screws.. they tend to break) and then seal it up with Liquid nails cunstruction adhesive. kinda overkill but if i have a leak with the amp i'm using my speaker will die as soon as it turn on the tunes! I've been doing this for about 6 years now and ony had one box fail... tried biscuit joints.. they don't work well under harsh vibrations and high pressure, such as in a car and with a sub shaking the crap out of it. <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 05-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Homicidal||05-09-2000||05:22 PM||||It's drying as we speak!  I talked to the Kragen guy and he reccomended a windshield silicone substance since it "flows" into crack and seals them.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||05-09-2000||08:29 PM||||construction adhesive works good if you can get it inside the box and push it out into the holes. (You can do it from outside but this is nasty stuff to clean up if you goof with it.)<P>Water proof, flexible, Very tough, will not shrink or crack. designed to glue panels (sheet rock, particle board or plywood) to beams, concrete, steel, or nearly anything else. Don't use it on plastic unless it's listed for plastic. Most of these eat plastic.<P>If I was building any kind of speaker enclosure I'd glue it with this stuff. You'll split the wood befor it come apart.||||reg||1
Z||000009||zeusrekning||05-09-2000||08:47 PM||||I built a wall in my 71 lemans a year or two ago and one of the enclosures sprung a leak and i could tell a big difference after i sealed it back. I used silicone but if your sub produces a lot of pressure and the leak is bad you'll probably leak again.If you build a box be sure to get all mating cuts as straight and smooth"flat" as possible. This was my problem. I built the whole \_/ shaped wall with a jig saw , circular saw, and a drill. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||overlord||05-10-2000||11:03 AM||||i just put on a can of "leak sealer" on my boxes...i remember seeing the add on tv a while ago..thought id try it.......ill check it out in a few hours see how it worked. I knew exactly where the leaks were so there will be no prob telling if the stuff worked or not.||||reg||1
Z||000011||zeusrekning||05-10-2000||07:50 PM||||Be sure to let it cure long enough. Like for silicone i would let it seal for at least a day.||||reg||1
Z||000012||overlord||05-11-2000||08:39 AM||||alrighty its been 24 since i put on theleak sealer.....cranked up the tunes and checked for the usual leaks....gone!!<P>this stuff goes on in a nice liquidy then hardens into kinda a silicone rubber stuff..<P>think i might put another can on just in case...and wow..what a difference when there are no air leaks.   i like it..yes i like it a lot.||||reg||1
